(THE LANTERN AT LOW TIDE) Tj
T*
(A short story written for Kenni) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The old harbor bell had not rung in years. It stood at the end of the pier,) Tj
T*
(green with salt, while the tide drew a silver seam around its stone.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Mara came there each evening with a lantern. Her grandfather had taught her) Tj
T*
(to light it before the fog arrived. A small light is still a direction, he) Tj
T*
(used to say.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(One autumn night the fog rolled in early. From beyond the breakwater came) Tj
T*
(three soft knocks: wood against wood. Then a voice called, too far away to) Tj
T*
(make out.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Mara lifted the lantern high. The fog swallowed its glow, but she kept it) Tj
T*
(there. A moment later, the harbor bell moved. Once. Twice. On the third) Tj
T*
(note, a little fishing boat emerged from the gray, turning toward shore.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Its sailor stepped onto the pier and thanked her. Mara looked at the bell,) Tj
T*
(still and quiet again. She understood then: the light had not made the fog) Tj
T*
(disappear. It had only shown someone where to come home.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(THE END) Tj
T*
() Tj
